WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:09.530
This is the story of a huge
change in the Chinese economy.

00:00:13.175 --> 00:00:20.990
A story about
hubris, about excess,

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:23.825
but above anything else,
it's a story about debts.

00:00:23.825 --> 00:00:25.857
Eye-watering
amounts of leverage.

00:00:25.857 --> 00:00:30.260
The rapid expansion of
China's property sector

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.500
on cheap credit.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.140
The meteoric rise
of a company founded

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.835
by one man who
ultimately thought

00:00:38.835 --> 00:00:40.460
that he could do
anything as long as he

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:41.510
borrowed enough money.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.390
This is a story of
the failure of one

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.790
of China's, and
also the world's,

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.785
largest real estate developers.

00:00:48.785 --> 00:00:50.480
A property developer
that came out

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:57.040
of absolutely nowhere and within
10 years had $150bn of assets.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.150
The most indebted
property company

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:02.300
in the world, Evergrande Group.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.920
If Evergrande
really goes under it

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.460
would definitely become China's
biggest ever bankruptcy case.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:17.290
This story goes to the
heart of China's position

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.785
as the prime locomotive
of global economic growth.

00:01:23.402 --> 00:01:26.770
For decades the property
sector has essentially

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:28.685
anchored China's economy.

00:01:28.685 --> 00:01:31.060
You can't underestimate how
important the property sector

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.615
is to China.

00:01:32.615 --> 00:01:35.920
A third of China's economic
output is in property.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:39.460
For other countries,
say US or UK,

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:41.710
the ratio would fall
below 20 per cent.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:43.660
The reason for that
is quite simply

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.640
one of the most
remarkable things that's

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:46.940
ever happened in human history.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:51.100
A great migration of people from
the countryside to the cities

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.210
of China.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:55.570
Hundreds of millions of sons,
and particularly daughters,

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.900
of Chinese farmers,
who moved from the farm

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.170
where they were earning
really the same as they

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:05.710
were earning 1,000 years
ago in Chinese history.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:08.110
Absolute subsistence wages.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:11.440
They travelled four or five
hours to the local city,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.500
and there they began working
in the big factories,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.195
the workshop of the world.

00:02:16.195 --> 00:02:19.450
In the 1990s, when Evergrande
was launched, about a third

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.950
of Chinese people
lived in cities.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:29.140
China's urban population
grew from around 415m

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:36.070
in 1998, when the housing
reform began, to more than 900m

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:38.492
as of the end of last year.

00:02:38.492 --> 00:02:40.840
Now there are more people
living in cities than there

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.270
are in the countryside.

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:47.080
Before 1998 every
Chinese urban citizen

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.985
would get a flat for
free from the government.

00:02:50.985 --> 00:02:53.890
People who lived in
government apartments

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:56.170
were allowed to buy
those apartments

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.570
at really a peppercorn amount.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:02.440
Chinese banks began issuing
mortgage loans and also

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.275
development loans to
property companies.

00:03:05.275 --> 00:03:08.530
Every single person that moves
from the countryside in China

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:11.564
to a city needs to
live in an apartment.

00:03:11.564 --> 00:03:13.397
And someone's got to
build those apartments.

00:03:13.397 --> 00:03:17.560
Construction workers, who mostly
work for property companies,

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.570
account for more
than 10 per cent

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:21.610
of China's total workforce.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:25.810
Land sales, which will
then feed into real estate,

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:29.410
account for almost a
third of local government

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:30.445
fiscal revenue.

00:03:30.445 --> 00:03:33.790
It created the perfect
conditions for the world's

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:35.185
biggest ever property boom.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.630
Evergrande was founded
in 1997 by Hui Ka Yan.

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.330
It was absolutely
at the right time.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:50.290
Hui Ka Yan was really good
at spotting that opportunity

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:52.397
and capitalising on this trend.

00:03:52.397 --> 00:03:55.180
Developers like Evergrande,
and there are dozens and dozens

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:59.110
of them, built China's
cities, in many cases

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:01.180
almost from scratch
to accommodate

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:05.520
this massive historic
flow of people.

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.630
Owning your own home after a
period of no property rights,

00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:12.870
I guess, is a massive
aspiration of the middle class.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.769
A middle class that now
numbers well over 400m people.

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:21.750
This was the absolute crucible
of China's economic boom

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:22.965
over the last two decades.

00:04:22.965 --> 00:04:24.930
Hui Ka Yan, he just rode it.

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:31.660
He just rode it from
the very beginning.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:34.405
He began building all kinds
of projects in Guangzhou.

00:04:34.405 --> 00:04:36.670
It's why it was able to
just borrow so heavily.

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:38.470
So it started from nothing.

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:42.767
Within 10 years it had
$150bn on its balance sheets.

00:04:42.767 --> 00:04:50.110
From 2004 to 2020 Evergrande's
total revenue grew

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:53.890
at an annualised
rate of 44 per cent,

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:57.595
the fastest growing companies
you would ever see in China.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:03.130
He's really been a poster boy
for how much money you can make

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:04.840
out of Chinese real estate.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.650
Hui spotted the opportunity
making small shoebox

00:05:08.650 --> 00:05:09.370
apartments.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:11.965
Instantly became a big success.

00:05:11.965 --> 00:05:17.620
In 2017 he shot to fame by
heading up the China rich list,

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.280
clocked $42.5bn.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.655
It's quite a departure
from his humble origins.

00:05:24.655 --> 00:05:27.280
People who've met him
say he's an extremely

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.860
charismatic person, an
extraordinary salesman.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:34.090
He likes to hire people from top
tier universities in the west

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:36.580
to be his personal
secretary, to make him

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.920
look like a westernised boss.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:40.870
He also was very
good at cultivating

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.430
political connections.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:44.590
You need those
connections in order

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:47.200
to get access to more credit.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.150
He cultivated very
good connections

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:53.680
with Joseph Lau, a
notorious Hong Kong tycoon,

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:59.080
whose company had lots of
investment in Evergrande.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:02.320
He was also part
of the delegation

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:08.530
for when Xi Jinping visited
Great Britain in 2015.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.690
You don't just
randomly get invited

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:13.043
to join those sort
of delegations.

00:06:13.043 --> 00:06:14.710
So it definitely made
the effort to make

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:15.877
those political connections.

00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:20.955
The chairman of Evergrande's
had a massive taste for luxury.

00:06:20.955 --> 00:06:23.820
He has lived this lifestyle
associated with newly minted

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:24.810
Chinese billionaires.

00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:27.390
He's got a luxury
house on the Peak.

00:06:27.390 --> 00:06:28.500
He had a superyacht.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:31.890
He was quite a
generous gift giver.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.160
He loved to mix with some of the
most important people in China,

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.185
in Hong Kong, even from the UK.

00:06:37.185 --> 00:06:41.520
In 2015 when his soccer
team, Guangzhou Evergrande,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:44.430
had won the Asia
Premiership, he had

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:50.640
Prince Andrew, Jack Ma over at
his hotel, popping champagne.

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:53.160
One of the ways in which he
sort of ingratiating himself

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:58.260
with some of these tycoons was
that he had these card nights.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:01.770
The folly of his
ambition is no better

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:05.880
seen than in this huge sprawling
plot in Hong Kong called

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.310
Project Castle,
where he intended

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:11.040
to build this Palace of
Versailles-style mansion

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.740
in one of the most overdeveloped
places in the world.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.740
Ten years on it's a
muddy plot of land.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:21.060
Evergrande went from nothing
to being this huge developer

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.660
with over 1,000 projects
in over 200 cities

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:25.920
across mainland China.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:29.520
Over two trillion renminbi of
assets on its balance sheet.

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:35.520
It's a business model that lends
itself to very rapid expansion.

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:38.820
Real estate developers
in China are buying land

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.425
from local governments.

00:07:40.425 --> 00:07:43.635
Local governments are a key
economic actor in China.

00:07:43.635 --> 00:07:45.510
They are the people who
decide whether or not

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:49.200
to build an industrial park
over here, a factory over there,

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:51.030
real estate
development somewhere.

00:07:51.030 --> 00:07:52.660
So these developers
buy their land,

00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:56.010
build apartments on the land,
and they sell the apartments

00:07:56.010 --> 00:07:58.290
to customers before
they've built them.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.930
The homebuyers need to
make in advanced payment.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:03.360
Evergrande has
allocated the funds

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.990
to other areas,
which, in turn, has

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:10.320
cost the projects to be
stalled, which would then

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.472
anger these homebuyers.

00:08:12.472 --> 00:08:14.490
This arms race between
multiple developers

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:17.740
encourages them to move
as quickly as possible.

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:21.690
Once you inject that into it,
the whole thing speeds up.

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:24.660
The Chinese government has tried
to control and constrain it,

00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:28.710
limiting the use of
onshore debt to buy land.

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:30.960
It's one reason that offshore
debt, international bond

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.510
markets, became so attractive.

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.840
But Evergrande took advantage
of seemingly limitless demand

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:42.662
from global investors for
Chinese real estate debt.

00:08:42.662 --> 00:08:44.370
It just seemed so
clear that it was going

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:47.145
to become too big to finance.

00:08:47.145 --> 00:08:49.590
The last two decades
in China have really

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:53.460
seen an unprecedented period
of economic growth, driven

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:55.470
by the property boom.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.600
But now a lot of people
are thinking that China

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:58.815
is running out of road.

00:08:58.815 --> 00:09:01.380
The growth rate of
urban population

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:04.620
has been above 3 per
cent or 4 per cent.

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:07.387
The rate last year has dropped.

00:09:07.387 --> 00:09:10.590
So that era of
very strong demand

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:14.910
in the cities for migrant
workers is largely over.

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:19.030
The ratio of empty apartments
in China could range between 10

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:23.040
per cent and as high as 40
per cent, or even 50 per cent

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:24.570
in some small cities.

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:27.330
There are enough
empty homes in China

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:31.200
right now to house
about 90m people,

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.320
greater than the population
of Germany or the UK.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:37.200
Newer developments were
built quite quickly

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:41.115
without a lot of consideration
for the logistics.

00:09:41.115 --> 00:09:43.380
I've travelled to
many parts of China,

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:48.150
where you go to a suburb and
suddenly you see this forest

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:49.290
of apartment blocks.

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.470
Carry on for five, 10 minutes
in your taxi, and you're

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:58.680
still going through
apartment block

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:01.500
after apartment block
with nobody in them.

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:05.250
I visited one of the most
famous ghost cities, Ordos,

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:08.850
in the middle of the desert,
very far away from anything.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:11.190
It was quite strange,
because it's not like there's

00:10:11.190 --> 00:10:12.945
necessarily jobs there.

00:10:12.945 --> 00:10:14.700
Housing affordability
in China is

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:16.720
among the worst in the world.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:21.000
It would take more than 50
years of median household

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:25.080
income in Beijing to buy
an average apartment.

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:28.800
The ratio is around
15 years in London

00:10:28.800 --> 00:10:30.420
and 10 years in New York.

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:32.610
Policymakers, led
by President Xi,

00:10:32.610 --> 00:10:34.335
wouldn't allow that to happen.

00:10:34.335 --> 00:10:38.550
They realised that you can't
keep on building property which

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:43.860
is often unsold by using
debt that property companies

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:46.110
ultimately will not
be able to repay.

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:48.300
Developers always
had a way to take

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:52.260
on more debt, which prompted
the three red lines policy.

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:54.600
In about August 2020
everyone was talking

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:56.047
about the three red lines.

00:10:56.047 --> 00:10:58.530
Three debt ratios
Chinese developers

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:02.425
must follow to gain
access to bank lending.

00:11:02.425 --> 00:11:06.400
First one is a liability to
asset ratio of less than 70

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:09.115
per cent, which is
essentially a way of avoiding

00:11:09.115 --> 00:11:09.990
too many liabilities.

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:12.510
Evergrande has over
$300bn of liabilities.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:15.700
The second one is a net
gearing ratio of less than 100

00:11:15.700 --> 00:11:16.200
per cent.

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:19.110
That's essentially a
way of measuring debt

00:11:19.110 --> 00:11:20.610
compared to equity.

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:23.400
And the third ratio is a
cash to short-term debt ratio

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:25.680
of more than one,
which just means

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:28.740
you've got more cash than
you've got short-term debt.

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:32.430
A vast majority of
Chinese major developers

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:34.950
were not able to meet
that requirement.

00:11:34.950 --> 00:11:37.005
They lost access to credit.

00:11:37.005 --> 00:11:40.650
It has changed the mindset
of lenders in China

00:11:40.650 --> 00:11:44.280
not to lend too much to
Evergrande and other property

00:11:44.280 --> 00:11:45.435
developers.

00:11:45.435 --> 00:11:49.200
At the same time the
government advised banks

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:54.400
to quietly slow down the
issuance of mortgage loans.

00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.110
Evergrande can't
sell apartments.

00:11:56.110 --> 00:11:57.850
It also can't borrow from banks.

00:11:57.850 --> 00:11:59.277
How could it pay off the loans?

00:11:59.277 --> 00:12:00.610
And that's where the problem is.

00:12:04.805 --> 00:12:07.910
These three red lines
have created pretty much

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:13.137
a sudden stop in terms of using
debt to fund debt payments.

00:12:13.137 --> 00:12:14.720
A huge part of the
appeal of investing

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:16.280
in any Chinese
company is the belief

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:20.750
that the government is going to
bail out any prominent player

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:21.800
if things go wrong.

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:23.360
This is still a
country controlled

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:25.182
by the Communist party.

00:12:25.182 --> 00:12:29.810
China saw, with shock, the
western economies almost

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:33.980
collapse during and after
the 2008 financial crisis

00:12:33.980 --> 00:12:36.620
created by a
massive debt bubble.

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:40.250
It just couldn't afford to
shoulder that type of risk

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:41.222
itself.

00:12:41.222 --> 00:12:43.430
The government is taking a
particularly negative view

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:44.720
on excessive leverage.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:47.285
Evergrande is the poster
child for excessive leverage.

00:12:47.285 --> 00:12:49.940
The Chinese property market
is a bit like a game of Jenga.

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:55.517
The wealth management investors,
offshore bondholders, banks.

00:12:55.517 --> 00:12:58.100
You've got the local governments
that sells land to Evergrande

00:12:58.100 --> 00:13:00.385
who need it for financing.

00:13:00.385 --> 00:13:01.760
You've got the
central government

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:03.470
that's trying to control
leverage in the economy,

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:04.610
trying to control the economy.

00:13:04.610 --> 00:13:06.440
You've got ordinary citizens
who are buying homes

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:07.982
from Evergrande and
other developers.

00:13:07.982 --> 00:13:09.860
You've got construction
workers who rely

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:11.810
on Evergrande for their income.

00:13:11.810 --> 00:13:14.972
A whole supply chain
of companies, not only

00:13:14.972 --> 00:13:16.430
within China, but
outside of China,

00:13:16.430 --> 00:13:19.420
that are providing the goods and
services to build apartments.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:22.670
Some of these blocks have
been pulled out entirely,

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:26.810
making the tower sort of
teeter and list to one side.

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:28.400
One of the first
blocks, which has not

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:32.220
been pulled out entirely,
has been consumer demand.

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:35.150
The really big one that
was pulled out entirely,

00:13:35.150 --> 00:13:39.080
and made the tower list,
was the three red lines,

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:41.180
the ability of
property developers

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:44.120
to borrow in many
different forms.

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:47.150
It's not so much just that
Evergrande is a tower that's

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:48.040
ready to crumble.

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:50.150
In many ways it
already has crumbled.

00:13:50.150 --> 00:13:54.140
But the Chinese property machine
hasn't completely crumbled.

00:13:54.140 --> 00:13:56.735
This is really, in many
ways, only just beginning.

00:13:56.735 --> 00:13:59.720
We've got bank lending,
a Jenga block that's

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:05.420
still in the tower, and
commercial paper issuance still

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:07.010
in the tower.

00:14:07.010 --> 00:14:10.610
These are areas where
property developers would

00:14:10.610 --> 00:14:13.190
be fundamentally vulnerable.

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:15.980
If those Jenga blocks
were to be pulled out

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:19.550
it really would bring the
whole tower crashing down,

00:14:19.550 --> 00:14:21.890
and it would bring the
Chinese property market

00:14:21.890 --> 00:14:25.850
to its knees, many more
bankruptcies among property

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:27.998
developers all over the country.

00:14:27.998 --> 00:14:29.540
What we might be
seeing here are just

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:30.830
the kind of teething
pains of what

00:14:30.830 --> 00:14:33.380
happens when the most powerful
economic forces in the world

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:34.220
begin to unravel.

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:37.460
If that's true then this is
all the tip of the iceberg,

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:40.700
and we're not looking at
one Jenga tower collapsing.

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:43.520
We're looking at a
whole forest of towers.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:45.260
In the summer it
really looked like it

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:48.230
was going to default. Hundreds
of ongoing residential

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:51.620
projects, in hundreds of
cities across China, suddenly

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:52.790
ground to a halt.

00:14:52.790 --> 00:14:56.420
In September 2021 there was
a hugely crucial moment.

00:14:56.420 --> 00:14:59.378
Evergrande missed payments on
wealth management products.

00:14:59.378 --> 00:15:00.920
The wealth management
market in China

00:15:00.920 --> 00:15:03.030
is where ordinary people
can put their savings,

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:05.030
and the money is usually
going into real estate,

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:06.692
often to companies
like Evergrande.

00:15:06.692 --> 00:15:08.150
And these people,
who had invested,

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:09.690
hadn't got their money back.

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:12.020
They descended on the
headquarters of Evergrande

00:15:12.020 --> 00:15:14.030
in Shenzhen.

00:15:14.030 --> 00:15:16.490
We don't know where
to get our money.

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:21.590
And now we go to Shenzhen
government, and nobody help us.

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:23.150
This was the point
at which it went

00:15:23.150 --> 00:15:25.955
from it might be a crisis to,
well, it probably is a crisis.

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:34.430
About a week later they just
didn't pay an interest payment

00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:36.298
on one of their dollar bonds.

00:15:36.298 --> 00:15:38.090
It's the first time
that a Chinese property

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:39.770
developer of this
size has defaulted

00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:41.210
on its offshore debts.

00:15:41.210 --> 00:15:44.030
And the entire bond market for
the Chinese property sector

00:15:44.030 --> 00:15:46.760
collapses.

00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:49.440
These are some of the world's
biggest investment banks,

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:50.450
investment funds.

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:52.760
When it comes to investing
in Evergrande bonds

00:15:52.760 --> 00:15:55.370
everybody expects to
take a massive haircut.

00:15:55.370 --> 00:15:58.220
Other property
companies in China

00:15:58.220 --> 00:16:00.777
are going to really struggle
to raise any financing at all

00:16:00.777 --> 00:16:01.610
on offshore markets.

00:16:04.195 --> 00:16:07.690
People no longer trust
Chinese companies that say

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:10.850
we're issuing bonds offshore,
we expect to honour them.

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:12.550
That's all completely changed.

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:14.740
The offshore bondholders
ploughed $20bn

00:16:14.740 --> 00:16:17.830
into this company on the
basis that everyone believed

00:16:17.830 --> 00:16:20.590
that China just would never
let its property sector fall,

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:23.320
would never let its
construction sector fall.

00:16:23.320 --> 00:16:24.940
Some people might
panic over what

00:16:24.940 --> 00:16:28.120
it meant for the entire
global financial system.

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:32.200
There's a lot of talk about how
Evergrande may be restructured,

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:34.000
potentially allowed
to go bankrupt.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.730
The scale of Evergrande's
borrowing is so vast.

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:40.150
$300bn is 2 per cent of GDP.

00:16:40.150 --> 00:16:43.270
So the idea that there could be
any failure of a restructuring,

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:46.150
and the whole thing could go
into a complete liquidation,

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:49.330
one, would go on
for likely a decade,

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:51.280
and, two, would have
unimaginable consequences

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:53.380
on China's property
sector and its economy.

00:16:53.380 --> 00:16:56.605
Evergrande will probably
fail in some form.

00:16:56.605 --> 00:16:58.630
It would be the
biggest bankruptcy

00:16:58.630 --> 00:16:59.818
ever in China's history.

00:16:59.818 --> 00:17:02.110
Investors around the world
have been incredibly spooked

00:17:02.110 --> 00:17:02.927
by Evergrande.

00:17:02.927 --> 00:17:05.349
It led to this sort
of domino effect.

00:17:05.349 --> 00:17:09.550
In the wake of Evergrande we've
had so many different defaults

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:12.700
and companies getting into
serious financial strife,

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:15.490
companies which have
international investors who

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:16.615
issued international bonds.

00:17:19.390 --> 00:17:22.540
There are comparisons that are
being made between the Lehman

00:17:22.540 --> 00:17:25.510
Brothers collapse, which
triggered the financial crisis

00:17:25.510 --> 00:17:28.164
in the US of 2008.

00:17:28.164 --> 00:17:30.100
The big question
everyone's been waiting

00:17:30.100 --> 00:17:32.020
for is when's the
next financial crisis.

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:34.570
The most indebted property
developer in China

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:37.000
defaults on its debt, it
sounds like there we are.

00:17:37.000 --> 00:17:38.065
We've got our crisis.

00:17:38.065 --> 00:17:40.210
We just don't know how
big the ripples will be,

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:41.950
which is why there's
so much uncertainty

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:43.620
and worry in global markets.

00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:47.170
The question now remains whether
the collapse of Evergrande

00:17:47.170 --> 00:17:52.167
will spread over to China's
financial industry in general.

00:17:52.167 --> 00:17:53.710
There are obvious similarities.

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:56.560
The Evergrande story is a story
about massive unmanageable

00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:57.265
debts.

00:17:57.265 --> 00:18:00.580
I don't think we're going
to see a financial market

00:18:00.580 --> 00:18:02.650
meltdown in China
of the type that we

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:05.382
saw in the aftermath
of the Lehman crisis.

00:18:05.382 --> 00:18:07.090
The Chinese government
will do everything

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:08.980
to contain the fallout.

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:13.765
That is a really key difference
between that and 2008.

00:18:13.765 --> 00:18:16.750
The situation with the Chinese
crisis is very, very different.

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:18.280
Everyone knew this
stuff was risky.

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:20.100
These are high yield bonds.

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.780
So many people had called
this as a disaster waiting

00:18:22.780 --> 00:18:26.290
to happen that it seems crazy
that the offshore bondholders

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:28.340
did put so much cash
into the company.

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:32.380
Massive investors from BlackRock
to Goldman Sachs to HSBC,

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:34.420
their funds invested
in these bonds.

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:36.220
They ploughed
billions of dollars

00:18:36.220 --> 00:18:37.900
into an already
overheated market

00:18:37.900 --> 00:18:39.733
because they believed
that China would never

00:18:39.733 --> 00:18:41.980
let its construction or
its property sector fall.

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:43.540
Turned out to be nonsense.

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:47.110
Whether or not they're able to
effectively restructure does

00:18:47.110 --> 00:18:49.870
reflect whether or
not, in the future,

00:18:49.870 --> 00:18:51.825
investors will have
that confidence.

00:18:51.825 --> 00:18:53.440
One of the big
unanswered questions

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:56.050
in the collapse of Evergrande
is, where were its auditors?

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:57.490
Where were PwC?

00:18:57.490 --> 00:19:01.575
They had a Big Four audit
firm auditing them for years,

00:19:01.575 --> 00:19:03.700
signing off their accounts,
signing off the figures

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:05.890
that management had
presented to them.

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:09.160
And then, suddenly, halfway
through 2021, Evergrande

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:10.780
tells its investors
that it might not

00:19:10.780 --> 00:19:12.730
be able to meet its
financial obligations.

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:14.710
The audit regulator in
Hong Kong has already

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:16.930
launched an investigation
into PwC's work.

00:19:18.720 --> 00:19:20.470
Evergrande has
become this symbol

00:19:20.470 --> 00:19:23.605
of this critical juncture
in China's economic story.

00:19:23.605 --> 00:19:29.500
It changes the outlook for
China as the world's locomotive

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:30.955
of economic growth.

00:19:30.955 --> 00:19:33.910
Building bridges, building
houses, building entire cities

00:19:33.910 --> 00:19:36.230
has been such a profound
driver of growth,

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:38.610
not just for China, but for
the entire global economy.

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:42.550
It is a very big
moment for a rethinking

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:46.480
by Chinese policymakers of
the reliance on property

00:19:46.480 --> 00:19:47.970
to fuel economic growth.

00:19:47.970 --> 00:19:51.790
According to the IMF, China's
contribution to global GDP

00:19:51.790 --> 00:19:56.590
growth, between
2013 and 2018, was

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:59.830
more than double the
contribution made by the United

00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:01.420
States in the same period.

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:05.440
A small fluctuation, a few
percentage points, in China

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:09.010
can have an impact
all over the world.

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:11.020
And it is a few
percentage points

00:20:11.020 --> 00:20:14.980
that Evergrande is now chilling
the Chinese economy by.

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:25.590
It's difficult to overstate the
spell that Evergrande helped

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:27.855
cast over property in China.

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:33.540
It's been a byword
for a property sector

00:20:33.540 --> 00:20:36.480
that couldn't fail, prices
that were always going to rise.

00:20:39.037 --> 00:20:40.948
Thousands of ordinary
Chinese citizens

00:20:40.948 --> 00:20:43.365
have made investments in the
company and bought its homes,

00:20:43.365 --> 00:20:46.140
but it remains to be
seen how many of them

00:20:46.140 --> 00:20:47.945
will get the homes
that they have

00:20:47.945 --> 00:20:49.320
been promised and,
in many cases,

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:50.640
paid upfront for, in full.

00:20:53.245 --> 00:20:56.860
It doesn't look good to
have people running around

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:58.990
in extreme wealth
at the same time

00:20:58.990 --> 00:21:01.630
when you're trying to
reassure the middle class

00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:03.470
that you're looking
after their interests.

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:06.790
It is a monumental
fall from grace.

00:21:06.790 --> 00:21:08.410
I think it's lost trust.

00:21:08.410 --> 00:21:09.835
I think it's lost
its credibility.

00:21:09.835 --> 00:21:12.002
We see it time and again
across China at the moment,

00:21:12.002 --> 00:21:15.430
billionaires stripped
of the luxury lifestyles

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:16.635
that they lead.

00:21:16.635 --> 00:21:19.930
The Evergrande fallout
is a pivotal moment

00:21:19.930 --> 00:21:22.210
for China's economic model.

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:25.060
It might be seen as
the inflection point

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:29.140
where China's growth model
shifted from one thing

00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:32.215
to something
completely different.

00:21:32.215 --> 00:21:34.930
Greener growth investment
in green industries,

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:36.910
an economy that's
more stable, that's

00:21:36.910 --> 00:21:39.882
less susceptible
to external shocks.

00:21:39.882 --> 00:21:41.590
Evergrande matters
because China matters.

00:21:41.590 --> 00:21:44.170
And Evergrande has
become this symbol

00:21:44.170 --> 00:21:47.275
of this critical juncture
in China's economic story.

00:21:47.275 --> 00:21:51.430
We've had two decades of
go-go growth, created largely

00:21:51.430 --> 00:21:54.640
by the booming property sector,
and now this really marks

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:56.095
the end of that boom.

00:21:56.095 --> 00:21:59.890
You can't just grow the economy
by borrowing, borrowing,

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:01.920
and borrowing.

